.json" "file_sha256" "f4385d0f6702ca6f8197ed3cc32c7f1c79cd2ae6f62bf5f1f7e599e97c1288a7" "basis" 1147 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-15327" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{e}^{-{x}^{2}/4}}\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4385d0f6702ca6f8197ed3cc32c7f1c79cd2ae6f62bf5f1f7e599e97c1288a7" "basis" 1147 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-346846" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Compute the arc length $L$ of $y=\\frac{1}{3}{({x}^{2}+2)}^{3/2}$ for $x\\in [2,5].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$ \n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4385d0f6702ca6f8197ed3cc32c7f1c79cd2ae6f62bf5f1f7e599e97c1288a7" "basis" 1147 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-346846" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{1}{3}{({x}^{2}+2)}^{3/2},$ $a=2,$ and $b=5.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{({x}^{2}+2)}^{1/2}\\cdot 2x=x\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+2)}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{[x\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+2)}]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}={x}^{2}+1$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+x)|}_{2}^{5} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}(5)^{3}+(5)]-[\\frac{1}{3}(2)^{3}+(2)] \\\\  & =42.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4385d0f6702ca6f8197ed3cc32c7f1c79cd2ae6f62bf5f1f7e599e97c1288a7" "basis" 1147 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-346846" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4e4b183965c62a7c75bfaa462ee8efd70fbadef48beebfed1b114ec3ed6029cf" "basis" 1147 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-346846" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4385d0f6702ca6f8197ed3cc32c7f1c79cd2ae6f62bf5f1f7e599e97c1288a7" "basis" 1147 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-352925" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Compute the arc length of $y=\\frac{{e}^{3x}+{e}^{-3x}}{6}$ for $x\\in [0,1].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4385d0f6702ca6f8197ed3cc32c7f1c79cd2ae6f62bf5f1f7e599e97c1288a7" "basis" 1147 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-352925" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{{e}^{3x}+{e}^{-3x}}{6},$ $a=0,$ and $b=1.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{e}^{3x}-{e}^{-3x}}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{{e}^{3x}-{e}^{-3x}}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{e}^{6x}-2+{e}^{-6x}}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{e}^{6x}+2+{e}^{-6x}}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{({e}^{3x}+{e}^{-3x})^{2}}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3x}+{e}^{-3x}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\frac{{e}^{3x}+{e}^{-3x}}{2}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{e}^{3x}+{e}^{-3x}}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{e}^{3x}-{e}^{-3x}}{6})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{e}^{3(1)}-{e}^{-3(1)}}{6}]-[\\frac{{e}^{3(0)}-{e}^{-3(0)}}{6}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}({e}^{3}-\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{6}-1}{6{e}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4385d0f6702ca6f8197ed3cc32c7f1c79cd2ae6f62bf5f1f7e599e97c1288a7" "basis" 1147 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-352925" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4e4b183965c62a7c75bfaa462ee8efd70fbadef48beebfed1b114ec3ed6029cf" "basis" 1147 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-352925" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4385d0f6702ca6f8197ed3cc32c7f1c79cd2ae6f62bf5f1f7e599e97c1288a7" "basis" 1147 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-352925" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{e^3-e^{-3}}{6}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4385d0f6702ca6f8197ed3cc32c7f1c79cd2ae6f62bf5f1f7e599e97c1288a7" "basis" 1147 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-6003" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4385d0f6702ca6f8197ed3cc32c7f1c79cd2ae6f62bf5f1f7e599e97c1288a7" "basis" 1147 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-6003" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4-{x}^{2}}\\,⟹\\,{f}^{′}(x)=\\pm \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm \\int \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm (-\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}})+C \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y=-\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}+\\sqrt{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4385d0f6702ca6f8197ed3cc32c7f1c79cd2ae6f62bf5f1f7e599e97c1288a7" "basis" 1147 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-6003" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4e4b183965c62a7c75bfaa462ee8efd70fbadef48beebfed1b114ec3ed6029cf" "basis" 1147 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-6003" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4385d0f6702ca6f8197ed3cc32c7f1c79cd2ae6f62bf5f1f7e599e97c1288a7" "basis" 1147 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-6003" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=-3\\sqrt{2-x}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4385d0f6702ca6f8197ed3cc32c7f1c79cd2ae6f62bf5f1f7e599e97c1288a7" "basis" 1147 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-6003" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=-\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}+\\sqrt{3}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4385d0f6702ca6f8197ed3cc32c7f1c79cd2ae6f62bf5f1f7e599e97c1288a7" "basis" 1147 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-6003" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=2\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}-x+1"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4385d0f6702ca6f8197ed3cc32c7f1c79cd2ae6f62bf5f1f7e599e97c1288a7" "basis" 1147 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-6003" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}+\\sqrt{3}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4385d0f6702ca6f8197ed3cc32c7f1c79cd2ae6f62bf5f1f7e599e97c1288a7" "basis" 1147 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-6003" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=\\sqrt{2-x}+\\sqrt{2}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4385d0f6702ca6f8197ed3cc32c7f1c79cd2ae6f62bf5f1f7e599e97c1288a7" "basis" 1147 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-82615" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{1+4{sec}^{4}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4385d0f6702ca6f8197ed3cc32c7f1c79cd2ae6f62bf5f1f7e599e97c1288a7" "basis" 1147 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-82615" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{1+4{sec}^{4}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}=4{sec}^{4}⁡(2x)\\,⟹\\,{f}^{′}(x)=\\pm 2{sec}^{2}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm 2\\int {sec}^{2}⁡(2x)\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm \\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)+1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4385d0f6702ca6f8197ed3cc32c7f1c79cd2ae6f62bf5f1f7e599e97c1288a7" "basis" 1147 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-82615" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4e4b183965c62a7c75bfaa462ee8efd70fbadef48beebfed1b114ec3ed6029cf" "basis" 1147 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-82615" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4385d0f6702ca6f8197ed3cc32c7f1c79cd2ae6f62bf5f1f7e599e97c1288a7" "basis" 1147 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-82615" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=-2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4385d0f6702ca6f8197ed3cc32c7f1c79cd2ae6f62bf5f1f7e599e97c1288a7" "basis" 1147 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-82615" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(4x)-1"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4385d0f6702ca6f8197ed3cc32c7f1c79cd2ae6f62bf5f1f7e599e97c1288a7" "basis" 1147 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-82615" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=2{tan}^{2}⁡(2x)-1"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4385d0f6702ca6f8197ed3cc32c7f1c79cd2ae6f62bf5f1f7e599e97c1288a7" "basis" 1147 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-82615" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=4{sec}^{2}⁡(2x)+1"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4385d0f6702ca6f8197ed3cc32c7f1c79cd2ae6f62bf5f1f7e599e97c1288a7" "basis" 1147 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-82615" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)+1"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4385d0f6702ca6f8197ed3cc32c7f1c79cd2ae6f62bf5f1f7e599e97c1288a7" "basis" 1147 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-82615" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)+1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4385d0f6702ca6f8197ed3cc32c7f1c79cd2ae6f62bf5f1f7e599e97c1288a7" "basis" 1147 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-7268" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the arc length of the curve $y = \\arccos{x}$ on $\\left[0,\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right]?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/example-7268.json" "file_sha256" "034b1652fbb8204927408d88692e8ee063626c2ce206b060635918fd80a078df" "basis" 1147 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-7268" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int _a^b \\sqrt{1 + [f'(x)]^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)= \\arccos{x} ,$ $a=0,$ and $b=\\dfrac{1}{2}.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf'(x) =-\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f'(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{1+{[{f}^{′}(x)]}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{\\frac{1-{x}^{2}+1}{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{\\frac{2-{x}^{2}}{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/example-7268.json" "file_sha256" "034b1652fbb8204927408d88692e8ee063626c2ce206b060635918fd80a078df" "basis" 1147 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-560" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Compute the arc length of  the curve $y=x^{3/2}$ between the points $x=0$ and $x=\\dfrac{4}{3},$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/assets/4ed8b94a4fff3c22697264e467f955f2e51a62618d03295c4e4f38493bff2ced.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/example-560.json" "file_sha256" "77b631252b6483555a202b99c2e74774b9acc0947d26e35e05d031f3c2398163" "basis" 1147 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-560" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int _a^b \\sqrt{1 + [f'(x)]^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)= x^{3/2},$ $a=0,$ and $b=\\dfrac{4}{3}.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf'(x) =   \\dfrac{3}{2}x^{1/2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt { 1 + [f'(x)]^2 }\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+{[{f}^{′}(x)]}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{[\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{1/2}]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{9}{4}x} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4+9x}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}(4+9x)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}}\\sqrt{4+9x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{4+9x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt { 1 + [f'(x)]^2 } = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\sqrt{4 + 9x}$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+{[{f}^{′}(x)]}^{2}}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{4}{3}}\\sqrt{4+9x}\\,dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate this integral using the substitution $u= 4+9x.$ Then, $\\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{1}{9}\\textrm{d}u.$\n\nWe also need to compute new limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ u & 4 & 16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{4}{3}}\\sqrt{4+9x}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{4}^{16}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{9}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}{\\int }_{4}^{16}{u}^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}{[\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{\\frac{3}{2}}]}_{4}^{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{[{u}^{\\frac{3}{2}}]}_{4}^{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}([(16)^{\\frac{3}{2}}]-[(4)^{\\frac{3}{2}}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}([64]-[8]) \\\\  & =\\frac{56}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/example-560.json" "file_sha256" "77b631252b6483555a202b99c2e74774b9acc0947d26e35e05d031f3c2398163" "basis" 1147 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-561" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\displaystyle  \\int_{5}^{6} } \\sqrt{1+\\dfrac {x^2}{x^2-16}}  \\,\\textrm{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve? \n\n- $y=  \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2-16}}$\n\n- $y=  \\sqrt{x^2-16} + \\sqrt 5$\n\n- $y=  \\dfrac{x}{x^2-16} + 1$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/example-561.json" "file_sha256" "946a15820022627309512b7650643ac5eab28a02f221c38e68cd24904da31930" "basis" 1147 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-561" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int _a^b \\sqrt{1 + [f'(x)]^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL=\\int_{5}^{6}  \\sqrt{1+\\dfrac {x^2}{x^2-16}}  \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[f'(x)]^2=\\dfrac {x^2}{x^2-16}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad f'(x) = \\pm \\dfrac {x}{\\sqrt{x^2-16}}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm \\int \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-16}}\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-16}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y=  \\sqrt{x^2-16} + \\sqrt 5.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/example-561.json" "file_sha256" "946a15820022627309512b7650643ac5eab28a02f221c38e68cd24904da31930" "basis" 1147 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-5985" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\int }_{3}^{5}\\sqrt{1+25{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4c768da4efb408121081183c2faa4f28e3a3934e391b52a1eb499e0f24a2c5e" "basis" 1202 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-5985" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{3}^{5}\\sqrt{1+25{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}=25{x}^{4}\\,⟹\\,{f}^{′}(x)=\\pm 5{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm 5\\int {x}^{2}\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{5}{3}{x}^{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y=-\\frac{5}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{11}{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4c768da4efb408121081183c2faa4f28e3a3934e391b