14029846/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-5985" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=5{x}^{2}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4c768da4efb408121081183c2faa4f28e3a3934e391b52a1eb499e0f24a2c5e" "basis" 1202 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-5985" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=5{x}^{5}+\\frac{9}{5}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4c768da4efb408121081183c2faa4f28e3a3934e391b52a1eb499e0f24a2c5e" "basis" 1202 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-5985" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=\\frac{5}{3}{x}^{3}+x+\\frac{12}{5}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4c768da4efb408121081183c2faa4f28e3a3934e391b52a1eb499e0f24a2c5e" "basis" 1202 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-5985" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=-\\frac{5}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{11}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4c768da4efb408121081183c2faa4f28e3a3934e391b52a1eb499e0f24a2c5e" "basis" 1202 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122390" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{(2-x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4c768da4efb408121081183c2faa4f28e3a3934e391b52a1eb499e0f24a2c5e" "basis" 1202 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122390" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{(2-x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}=\\frac{1}{(2-x)^{2}}\\,⟹\\,{f}^{′}(x)=\\pm \\frac{1}{2-x}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm \\int \\frac{1}{2-x}\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm (-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-x))+C \\\\  & =∓\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-x)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-x)+1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4c768da4efb408121081183c2faa4f28e3a3934e391b52a1eb499e0f24a2c5e" "basis" 1202 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122390" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9a7cb11605217c1c6bb1b419f93461fc14a60161bb4fe0cc680ff39d9347b1e3" "basis" 1202 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122390" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4c768da4efb408121081183c2faa4f28e3a3934e391b52a1eb499e0f24a2c5e" "basis" 1202 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122390" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-x)+1"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4c768da4efb408121081183c2faa4f28e3a3934e391b52a1eb499e0f24a2c5e" "basis" 1202 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122390" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-x)+1"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4c768da4efb408121081183c2faa4f28e3a3934e391b52a1eb499e0f24a2c5e" "basis" 1202 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122390" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4c768da4efb408121081183c2faa4f28e3a3934e391b52a1eb499e0f24a2c5e" "basis" 1202 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122390" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+x)+1"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4c768da4efb408121081183c2faa4f28e3a3934e391b52a1eb499e0f24a2c5e" "basis" 1202 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122390" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+1"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4c768da4efb408121081183c2faa4f28e3a3934e391b52a1eb499e0f24a2c5e" "basis" 1202 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-15263" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038326/assets/3e2aa5bcd84bb8290613d937d75a412869589fd9690f1bd8fe1754faa4ea9f36.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve $y=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9}$ on $[-2,3],$ as shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038326/state.json" "file_sha256" "ea45abb7e713b02bb4729ce423c94b9bb7fa668ec61b7ffb9c7d5b92873b8acf"} {"question" "q-15263" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9},$ $a=-2,$ and $b=3.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{9}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{-2}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{[{f}^{′}(x)]}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{3}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{9}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-2}^{3}\\sqrt{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038326/state.json" "file_sha256" "ea45abb7e713b02bb4729ce423c94b9bb7fa668ec61b7ffb9c7d5b92873b8acf"} {"question" "q-15263" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038326/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4262bf588e027a0d6e7f55fddfebd4dbac0c3fff95882947208ffba7b7cbd297"} {"question" "q-15263" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038326/state.json" "file_sha256" "ea45abb7e713b02bb4729ce423c94b9bb7fa668ec61b7ffb9c7d5b92873b8acf"} {"question" "q-15263" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-2}^{3}\\sqrt{3{x}^{4}+1}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-2}^{3}\\sqrt{{x}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-2}^{3}\\sqrt{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-2}^{3}\\sqrt{9{x}^{4}+1}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-2}^{3}\\sqrt{{x}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038326/state.json" "file_sha256" "ea45abb7e713b02bb4729ce423c94b9bb7fa668ec61b7ffb9c7d5b92873b8acf"} {"question" "q-15263" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-2}^{3}\\sqrt{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038326/state.json" "file_sha256" "ea45abb7e713b02bb4729ce423c94b9bb7fa668ec61b7ffb9c7d5b92873b8acf"} {"question" "q-15315" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following gives the length of the curve $y=2\\sqrt{x}$ on $[0,1]?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038326/state.json" "file_sha256" "ea45abb7e713b02bb4729ce423c94b9bb7fa668ec61b7ffb9c7d5b92873b8acf"} {"question" "q-15315" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=2\\sqrt{x},$ $a=0,$ and $b=1.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{2}{2\\sqrt{x}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+{[{f}^{′}(x)]}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{\\frac{1+x}{x}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038326/state.json" "file_sha256" "ea45abb7e713b02bb4729ce423c94b9bb7fa668ec61b7ffb9c7d5b92873b8acf"} {"question" "q-15315" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Develop